#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199320} :activity/id #uuid "342a691e-fc66-56ce-aabf-098ff3242cb2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049315 :topic/id #uuid "eba98dcb-66f5-5f67-ad46-4c466f76f5ef" :topic/math-academy-id 4162} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198390 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198032 :question/id #uuid "5ff4ed47-dffe-534e-baf9-41bded65d457" :question/math-academy-id "e-12385"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050188}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362527}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"} :step/id #uuid "f24da8a5-d03b-52d4-93ed-bd3587f66dfa" :step/math-academy-id 44438 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199322}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198391 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198033 :question/id #uuid "06cccfa3-4b70-5e67-9e9f-9f5c83b319bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-12386"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050966}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278441}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "bfcf599a-5392-50e6-bb70-aea6cfbb105a" :step/math-academy-id 44442 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199469 :tutorial/content "![](images/d2/d2a5641e502b11cca4f469ec743adc62d614f669d61c1b2d0b0c7549fb60bf6b.png)\n\nGiven a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin, an outward-pointing unit normal vector to the sphere at the point $P(x,y,z)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can sometimes use this to calculate the flux of a vector field through a sphere. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "6dc7d279-b10e-5ca3-b496-0382ff5c50ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7652 :tutorial/title "The Outward-Pointing Unit Vector to a Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "2deaec25-cf1d-52dd-97eb-efa9bad2cd8f" :step/math-academy-id 44445 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199479 :tutorial/content "Similar to the case of *closed* curves, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" a closed *surface*. \n\nFor closed surfaces, the convention is the positive orientation is always given by the *outward-pointing* unit normal vector.\n\n![](images/d2/d2bb07e4684cfc3a8c5641ecc6930dbc1c76027a735c7efe0c1c60cd17a91060.png)\n\nWhen calculating the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a closed surface, we always measure the flux relative to the surface's positive orientation.  \n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9a27f82e-a934-5e25-87cc-8140ba349eae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7726 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "70493dbd-ab3f-5404-8591-79605bde33e1" :step/math-academy-id 44783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199480 :tutorial/content "Let's derive the formula for a unit normal vector for a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin. \n\nConsider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - r^2\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = r^2.$ \n\nConsider the point $P(x,y,z)$ that lies on $S.$ Then, $\\nabla f(x,y,z)$ is a normal vector to the level surface at the point $P$.\n\nComputing $\\nabla f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨{f}_{x},\\,{f}_{y},\\,{f}_{z}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P(x,y,z)$ lies on $S,$ we have $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = r^2.$\nNormalizing $\\nabla f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{∇f(x,y,z)}{‖∇f(x,y,z)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit normal vector for a sphere of radius $r$ centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "51218e5e-009c-5c0c-8034-3440e9b736c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7729 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Unit Normal Vector for a Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "c5b99d56-2b0a-5e62-9a25-4853c59d5bab" :step/math-academy-id 44787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198404 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198046 :question/id #uuid "8d046f27-886d-5342-8868-e2c3ae731fa5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12453"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281495}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"} :step/id #uuid "7203bf6c-a682-5850-807b-ad02403f986a" :step/math-academy-id 44792 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199298}}] :activity/title "Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198493}]}
